WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:04.640
That’s a loon.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.181
And right now, he’s doing something 
called wailing.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.263
But this is probably not the first time 
you’ve heard that noise.

00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:18.814
Here it is in the movie 1917, 
as they enter the enemy trench.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:25.544
In the third Harry Potter movie, as they 
climb out of the whomping willow.

00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:30.965
In Rick and Morty, when Rick uses 
his intergalactic toilet.

00:00:33.169 --> 00:00:36.419
You’ll find it whenever a scene 
needs to feel a little moody.

00:00:37.861 --> 00:00:38.629
"They feared it."

00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:39.790
Or eerie.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.393
Or just remind us that we’re in 
the great outdoors.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.579
This specific bird call is all over 
movies and tv shows.

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:51.473
But, it really shouldn’t be.

00:00:58.499 --> 00:01:04.139
If you ever find yourself on birder twitter,
you’ll find a lot of angry tweets like these:

00:01:04.139 --> 00:01:06.226
"Can someone please tell movie makers 

00:01:06.226 --> 00:01:09.248
that loons are not, in fact, 
the only bird out there?"

00:01:09.248 --> 00:01:13.380
"Hello I am a nature scene in a movie - doesn’t
matter whether it’s habitat appropriate

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.017
or not — I’m a Common Loon call."

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:21.550
"Have you ever noticed loon calls in impossible
locations in movies &amp; TV? ...in a jungle?"

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.250
SOHL: Watching the movie Harriet.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.188
Only 20 minutes in but I’m giving them kudos.

00:01:26.188 --> 00:01:27.430
They were not lazy!

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.143
No loon calls yet.

00:01:29.143 --> 00:01:32.410
Can you read the first reply on that one?

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.830
SOHL: Oh, myself?

00:01:35.421 --> 00:01:36.421
SOHL: No! Why, Harriet!

00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:40.495
At about the one hour 20 minute mark... they
throw in calling loons.

00:01:40.495 --> 00:01:41.380
That's Terry.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.670
He's a research geographer.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:48.177
But more importantly, he moonlights
on twitter and his blog, as DakotaBirder.

00:01:48.177 --> 00:01:51.165
He mostly posts beautiful pictures of birds.

00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:54.315
And points out when Hollywood 
uses their calls incorrectly.

00:01:54.315 --> 00:01:57.063
SOHL: As a birder you just you just can't--
you can't help but notice.

00:01:57.957 --> 00:02:01.276
That's because the loon 
is a very particular bird.

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:03.482
They’re also called “divers”.

00:02:03.482 --> 00:02:08.009
Because they’re really good at diving, 
thanks to their dense bones and enormous feet.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.098
They’re also good at something called 
penguin dancing.

00:02:11.098 --> 00:02:14.305
And scooting across lakes 
at astonishing speeds.

00:02:14.305 --> 00:02:16.634
This is a map of the common loon’s range:

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:20.560
They mostly stick to Canada and the northern
US -- where there are plenty of lakes for

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.730
them to hang out in.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.240
But, they do migrate.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.599
Passing through other US states on their way
to winter along the coasts

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:28.465
 as far down as Mexico.

00:02:28.465 --> 00:02:31.773
And here’s a very unscientific map 
of all of the places

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:34.745
I found a common loon call 
in movies and tv.

00:02:34.745 --> 00:02:40.602
SOHL: I certainly wouldn't expect a common
loon in Hawaii or in Bolivia or in South Africa.

00:02:41.165 --> 00:02:45.615
And geographic location isn’t the only thing
Hollywood gets wrong.

00:02:45.615 --> 00:02:48.125
A loon might be found in California.

00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:49.743
But not during the summer.

00:02:49.743 --> 00:02:51.456
Like Becky, in Finding Dory.

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:54.865
"Lads, meet Becky."

00:02:54.865 --> 00:02:59.042
And unlike what this clip from 
the TV show Rome implies...

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.909
...you'd never find a loon 
in the desert.

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:05.522
SOHL: No, they need water.

00:03:05.522 --> 00:03:07.519
And quite a lot of it.

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:11.944
SOHL: If you've ever seen a loon try to fly,
it takes them a little while to get going.

00:03:11.944 --> 00:03:15.000
They’re actually really clumsy on land.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.342
So, you also wouldn’t find them in 
the Vietnamese jungle.

00:03:18.342 --> 00:03:19.770
Like in the movie Platoon.

00:03:22.306 --> 00:03:26.023
And then there’s this clip, from 
Kubo and the Two Strings.

00:03:27.598 --> 00:03:29.310
"Golden heron."

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.741
SOHL: That happens a lot.

00:03:31.741 --> 00:03:36.969
it's one thing to use the wrong bird call
as a background noise 

00:03:36.969 --> 00:03:42.231
it's another to actually show a picture of 
the bird and use a wrong call.

00:03:42.231 --> 00:03:46.120
And even if those were loons 
that movie takes place in Japan.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.533
Which, is at least on planet Earth.

00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:50.509
Unlike Avengers: Infinity War.

00:03:50.509 --> 00:03:54.049
SOHL: The big baddie, Thanos, he returns to
his home planet.

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:56.653
SOHL: And so they pan to an overview of the
planet 

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.040
and the first noise you hear is a common loon.

00:04:02.039 --> 00:04:04.619
SOHL: It just ruined the moment.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:09.980
Some credit a 1981 movie called On Golden
Pond, as the first to use loon calls.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.060
"The loons!"

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:12.140
"The loons."

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.281
"Oh look, I've spotted the loons!"

00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:16.474
It did feature a lot of loons.

00:04:16.474 --> 00:04:18.315
But they used them correctly.

00:04:18.315 --> 00:04:20.675
It’s set up north, on a lake, 
during the summer.

00:04:21.901 --> 00:04:26.875
Really, it’s hard to tell which film kicked
off Hollywood's loon obsession or when.

00:04:26.875 --> 00:04:29.262
But it’s pretty easy to figure out why.

00:04:29.262 --> 00:04:31.170
SOHL: I mean, it is a scary sound.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.750
I mean, it is just weird.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:33.850
It's unusual.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.569
In some Algonquin lore, 
loons are messengers 

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:40.436
and supernatural companions 
to legendary hero, Glooskap.

00:04:40.436 --> 00:04:44.783
In 1864, naturalist slash philosopher 
Henry David Thoreau

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:47.941
said the loon’s call was 
“singularly human”.

00:04:47.941 --> 00:04:53.215
And in 1913, John Muir, another elaborately
bearded naturalist slash philosopher

00:04:53.215 --> 00:04:56.974
called it “strange, sad, mournful and unearthly”.

00:04:56.974 --> 00:04:59.915
But the loons aren’t sad 
when they’re wailing.

00:04:59.915 --> 00:05:01.480
They actually have four calls.

00:05:02.275 --> 00:05:03.701
There’s the hoot.

00:05:03.701 --> 00:05:04.680
The yodel.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:06.739
The tremolo.

00:05:07.485 --> 00:05:08.367
And the wail.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.255
These two are both used during stressful situations.

00:05:14.255 --> 00:05:17.118
When another bird is overstepping his bounds.

00:05:17.118 --> 00:05:19.235
Or when some other threat is looming.

00:05:19.798 --> 00:05:23.359
If a loon ever looks at you like this 
you should probably back away.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.837
The hoot is mostly for chatting 
to fellow loons up close.

00:05:27.837 --> 00:05:32.587
And the famous wail, it’s basically just
the loon equivalent of a game of Marco Polo.

00:05:32.587 --> 00:05:35.486
A way of saying 
“Hey! Where is everybody?”

00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:40.136
But to human ears even the calmest wail 
sounds remarkably melancholy.

00:05:43.202 --> 00:05:48.382
I’m no musician, so I enlisted a few of
my coworkers to help explain why.

00:05:48.382 --> 00:05:50.838
Like Dave, who’s editing this video.

00:05:50.838 --> 00:05:53.818
And Charlie, who hosts the podcast 
Switched on Pop.

00:05:53.818 --> 00:05:56.370
As well as Vox’s Creative Director, Joe.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.919
Who sent me this video of
his interpretation.

00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:05.165
JOE: Now let me show you 
what it looks like in Melodyne.

00:06:05.165 --> 00:06:09.889
They helped me turn the loon wail into one
of these, a chart showing pitch over time.

00:06:12.971 --> 00:06:16.810
In this section, you can see that the loon 
makes a jump between two notes.

00:06:18.335 --> 00:06:22.460
In complicated musical terms, 
this interval is close to a major third

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.248
but it also bends into a minor third 
right at the end.

00:06:26.872 --> 00:06:31.508
JOE: So it starts in a major and ends in a
minor which is extremely funky.

00:06:31.508 --> 00:06:35.853
And the loon doesn’t just jump from 
note to note like a lot of chirpy birds.

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:40.731
Instead, the notes sort of slide into each other
a feature that Charlie pointed out

00:06:40.731 --> 00:06:43.600
 you'll also hear in blues and soul music.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.853
Singers like BB King and Muddy Waters and
songs like "Feelin' Good" by Nina Simone.

00:06:48.853 --> 00:06:53.358
"Birds flying' high, you know how it feels"

00:06:53.358 --> 00:06:57.205
So it’s no wonder the loon wail 
is Hollywood’s favorite bird call.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:07:01.684
It's a shortcut to a feeling, enhancing a
moment's melancholy, or tension

00:07:01.684 --> 00:07:03.380
or sheer remoteness.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:08.817
SOHL: On the few occasions that I have heard
it in real life, it really is unforgettable.

00:07:08.817 --> 00:07:12.315
SOHL: It's just so unlike any other bird call.

00:07:12.315 --> 00:07:15.666
SOHL: It's hard that first time you hear it
not to have goose bumps.

00:07:15.666 --> 00:07:20.559
It just is such a haunting, unusual sound.

00:07:26.706 --> 00:07:29.440
There is a lot more to learn about loons.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.250
They return to the same lake year after year

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.428
They do this weird foot waggle 
and nobody really knows why.

00:07:35.428 --> 00:07:38.751
And they carry their cute, fluffy babies 
on their backs.

00:07:38.751 --> 00:07:42.383
And their populations are dwindling due to
pollution, human disturbance, 

00:07:42.383 --> 00:07:43.516
and climate change.

00:07:43.516 --> 00:07:48.194
According to one model, the loon is on track
to lose about half of it’s range by 2080.

00:07:48.194 --> 00:07:51.401
If you want to learn more about loons, or
what you can do to help protect them

00:07:51.401 --> 00:07:56.218
I've linked some organizations devoted to their
conservation in the description of this video.

